  "content": {
    "id": "p33-q3-node",
    "type": "question",
    "label": "3",
    "prompt": "Bob differentiates $y=(x^2+1)(3x+1)$ and writes $y'=2x\\times3=6x$. What did he do wrong? What should he do instead?",
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [],
    "answer": {
      "short": "Bob multiplied the derivatives of the two factors. He should use the product rule: $y'=2x(3x+1)+3(x^2+1)=9x^2+2x+3$.",
      "worked": "Bob differentiated each factor and multiplied the results. Using $u=x^2+1$ and $v=3x+1$ gives\n$$\\begin{align*}y'&=u'v+uv'\\\\y'&=2x(3x+1)+3(x^2+1)\\\\y'&=9x^2+2x+3.\\end{align*}$$",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
    },
    "answerSpaceMm": 44
  },
